# Cross-frame MIXED-category callee, caught by `catch Fault.DivByZero:` ONLY
# (error-model.md §11, Increment 2.1c — the §3 uncaught-category re-panic guard).
# `mixed` can raise BOTH an Overflow (`a * b`) and a DivByZero (`a / b`); the
# call site catches DivByZero ONLY. So:
#   - the FIRST call's DivByZero is CAUGHT → prints 777 (the slot-tag dispatch
#     routes the DivByZero tag to the user handler);
#   - the SECOND call OVERFLOWS (`a * b` traps before the divide) → the slot
#     carries the Overflow tag, which this scope does NOT catch → the caller
#     re-dispatches to the (always-Some) panic block → "integer overflow", exit 1.
# This proves: a category the scope doesn't catch does NOT silently fall through
# (a Core-#8 miscompile) — it re-panics, uniform across both backends.
int mixed(int a, int b): a * b + a / b     # both a faultable mul AND a faultable div

void main():
    int z = 0
    int caught = mixed(10, z) catch Fault.DivByZero: 777
    print(f"{caught}")                       # div0 caught → 777
    int big = 4611686018427387904            # 2^62; big*big overflows i64
    # Overflow is NOT caught by a DivByZero-only scope → re-panic "integer overflow".
    int r = mixed(big, big) catch Fault.DivByZero: 777
    print(f"{r}")                            # never reached (panic above)
